-- title:
--   Finite dual measures and positive definite functions
-- statement:
--   Let $V$ be an arbitrary real vector space and let $V^*$ be its algebraic real dual, equipped with the sigma-algebra generated by the scalar evaluations $\alpha\mapsto\alpha(v)$. A function $\chi:V\to\mathbb C$ is positive definite on the additive group of $V$ and continuous on every finite-dimensional subspace if and only if there is a unique finite positive measure $\mu$ on this dual such that
--
--   $$\chi(v)=\int_{V^*}e^{i\alpha(v)}\,d\mu(\alpha)\qquad(v\in V).$$
--
--   Positive definiteness means that every finite quadratic form $\sum_{i,j}\overline{c_i}c_j\chi(v_j-v_i)$ is real and nonnegative. Finite-dimensional continuity uses the usual Euclidean topology, equivalently continuity along every linear map from $\mathbb R^n$. This criterion includes the zero measure and does not impose $\chi(0)=1$. It supplies the finite-dimensional-to-dual measure interface.
-- source:
--   Reflection Positive Stochastic Processes Indexed by Lie Groups, arXiv:1510.07445v3, PDF p. 32, Theorem 5.2

import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Gap48_P06

noncomputable section

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter

open scoped Topology ENNReal NNReal

namespace QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Gap48.P06

/-- Theorem 5.2, PDF p.32: finite positive dual measures correspond exactly to
positive definite functions continuous on every finite-dimensional subspace.
Uniqueness is part of the conclusion. Printed/changed/why: evaluation V* -> V
is read as V* -> R, because alpha(v) is real; no probability normalization is
added. The scalar-codomain correction was independently checked in round 1. -/
theorem algebraicDual_fourier_characterization
    {V : Type*} [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℝ V] (chi : V → ℂ) :
    (AdditivePositiveDefinite chi ∧ FiniteDimensionalContinuous chi) ↔
      ∃! mu : @Measure (Module.Dual ℝ V) (dualEvaluationSigma V),
        @IsFiniteMeasure (Module.Dual ℝ V) (dualEvaluationSigma V) mu ∧
          ∀ v, dualFourier mu v = chi v := by sorry

end QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Gap48.P06

end
